---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Whole House Generators",
    "url": "/best-whole-house-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Generac Generator Cost",
    "url": "/generac-generator-cost/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Generac vs Kohler",
    "url": "/generac-vs-kohler/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Whole House Generator Installation Cost",
    "url": "/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Natural Gas Generators",
    "url": "/best-natural-gas-generator/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-05", modified: "2026-11-05" });
const title = "6 Best 26kW Generators in 2026: Standby Units Compared";
const description =
  "The 24-28kW air-cooled standby class compared: natural gas vs propane ratings, two-AC starting, load management, fuel use and installed cost.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "generac-7328-26kw",
    "name": "Generac 7328 26kW Home Standby Generator with 200A Transfer Switch",
    "category": "Next Gen Air-Cooled, 26kW LP / 24kW NG",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated 26kW on propane and 24kW on natural gas, a smaller NG derate than the older Guardian 26kW",
      "Bundled 200-amp service-entrance transfer switch with a factory-installed whole-home surge protection device",
      "Built-in cellular Mobile Link monitoring, so it does not depend on home Wi-Fi staying up",
      "Largest dealer network of any standby brand, which matters more than any spec when it needs service",
      "Generac lists 2.05 gal/hr of propane at half load"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "5-year limited warranty is shorter than Briggs & Stratton (7) or Champion (10)",
      "Some Mobile Link features need a paid subscription"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The default 26kW choice: the strongest natural gas rating in the Generac air-cooled range below 28kW, a service-rated switch in the box, and the easiest brand to get serviced.",
    "description": "The 7328 is the current Next Gen version of Generac's 26kW air-cooled unit, and the change that matters is on natural gas. Generac rates it at 26,000 watts on propane and 24,000 watts on natural gas, where the outgoing Guardian 7290/7291 was rated 22,500 watts on natural gas. On a house fed by a utility gas line, that 1,500-watt difference is roughly one more air conditioner compressor running, so check the model number on any quote. Generac's spec page lists fuel use of 182 cubic feet per hour of natural gas at half load and 316 at full load, or 2.05 and 3.95 gallons of propane per hour. The package includes a 200-amp service-entrance-rated automatic transfer switch, which retailers list with a factory-installed whole-home surge protection device, and the unit reports over a built-in cellular connection rather than home Wi-Fi. That last point is easy to dismiss until an outage takes the router down with it. Generac lists the generator at 524 pounds and covers it with a 5-year limited warranty, the shortest here. Retail listings at AP Electric and Lowe's put the 7328 package between roughly $7,250 and $8,200 before installation. It is the pick for most buyers because a Generac dealer is usually the closest one, and with a standby generator the service network is part of the product."
  },
  {
    "id": "kohler-26rcal-200sels",
    "name": "Kohler 26RCAL-200SELS 26kW Home Standby Generator",
    "category": "Air-Cooled, 26kW LP / 24kW NG, RXT Switch",
    "badge": "Best Build Quality",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "24kW on natural gas, matching the Generac 7328, from a 999cc Command PRO engine with hydraulic valve lifters",
      "Kohler's sheet lists 290 cfh of natural gas at full load, the lowest full-load figure on this page",
      "Warranty covers parts, labor and travel for 5 years or 2,000 hours",
      "RXT transfer switch includes a combined interface and load management board",
      "Kohler says the enclosure meets NFPA guidance for 18-inch clearance to combustibles"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Spec sheet lists 39 kVA of peak motor starting at a 35% dip, well below Briggs & Stratton's 65.5 kVA",
      "Fewer dealers than Generac in many regions"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one to buy if you can get it serviced locally: the most frugal full-load fuel figure here and a warranty that pays the technician's travel.",
    "description": "Kohler's 26RCAL is the 26RCA generator packaged with a 200-amp service-entrance Model RXT automatic transfer switch, and the spec sheet (G4-315) rates the single-phase unit at 26kW on LPG and 24kW on natural gas, or 109 and 100 amps at 240 volts. The engine is Kohler's own CH1006 Command PRO, a 999cc V-twin with hydraulic valve lifters, so there is no periodic valve adjustment to pay for. Fuel use is where it stands out on paper: Kohler lists 290 cubic feet per hour of natural gas at full load and 180 at half load, and 129 cubic feet per hour of propane at full load, which works out to about 3.5 gallons per hour using Kohler's own 36.39 cubic feet per gallon conversion. Kohler rates normal operation at 67 dB(A) at 7 meters and the weekly exercise at 56 dB(A), and the default exercise runs for just 90 seconds. The RXT switch carries a load management board, so shedding an air conditioner or a range is part of the package rather than an add-on. The number to know before buying is motor starting: Kohler lists 39 kVA of peak motor starting at a 35% voltage dip on the single-phase unit, against 65.5 kVA on the Briggs & Stratton sheet. On a house with two large compressors, that makes the load management board earn its keep. The warranty is 5 years or 2,000 hours and covers parts, labor and travel."
  },
  {
    "id": "briggs-stratton-powerprotect-040806",
    "name": "Briggs & Stratton PowerProtect PP26 040806 26kW Standby Generator",
    "category": "Air-Cooled, 26kW LP / 24kW NG, Unit Only",
    "badge": "Best for Motor Starting",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Spec sheet lists 65.5 kVA of motor starting capability at a 35% voltage dip, the highest figure we found in the class",
      "7-year limited warranty on the generator, parts, labor and travel",
      "Rated 65 dB(A) in normal operation and 63 dB(A) in low-idle mode at 7 meters",
      "24kW on natural gas from the NGMax-tuned Vanguard 993cc V-twin",
      "Enclosure rated for 186 mph winds"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Highest fuel use on this page: 4.70 gal/hr of propane and 323 cfh of natural gas at full load",
      "040806 is the generator only, so the transfer switch is a separate line item"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for houses with two big compressors or a well pump: the strongest published motor-starting figure and the longest warranty of the big three brands.",
    "description": "The PP26 is Briggs & Stratton's 26kW PowerProtect unit, and the 040806 spec sheet rates it at 26kW on propane and 24kW on natural gas, or 108.3 and 100 amps. The figure that separates it from the others is motor starting: Briggs lists 65.5 kVA at a 35% voltage dip. That is the number that tells you whether two air conditioners, or an air conditioner and a well pump, can start without the lights sagging hard, and no other spec sheet we read for this class publishes a higher one. The engine is a Vanguard M61, a 993cc V-twin with an aluminum block and cast iron sleeves, and the enclosure is aluminum with a 186 mph wind rating. Briggs rates it at 65 dB(A) in normal operation and 63 dB(A) in low-idle mode, measured at 7 meters. The cost of that headroom is fuel. Briggs lists 206 cubic feet per hour of natural gas at half load and 323 at full load, and 2.60 gallons of propane per hour at half load and 4.70 at full load, the highest consumption of the six picks. On a propane tank that matters, on a utility gas line much less. The warranty is 7 years on parts, labor and travel. The 040806 is the generator alone; Briggs sells PowerProtect bundles with transfer switches, so confirm with your dealer which switch is being quoted. Briggs standby generators go through authorized dealers rather than Amazon."
  },
  {
    "id": "champion-201614-26kw-flex",
    "name": "Champion 201614 26kW fleX Home Standby Generator with 200A Switch",
    "category": "Air-Cooled, 26kW LP / 23.4kW NG, 200A SE Switch",
    "badge": "Longest Warranty",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "10-year limited warranty on the generator, the longest here",
      "Rated 26kW on propane and 23.4kW on natural gas",
      "200-amp service-entrance switch with an adjustable response time of 2.5 to 240 seconds",
      "Wi-Fi Direct setup on the transfer switch",
      "Retailer listings near $7,499 for the full package with switch"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Newest product line here, with the smallest service network and the least owner history",
      "Transfer switch warranty is only 2 years"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The warranty play. Ten years of coverage is a real hedge on an air-cooled engine, but find a local Champion standby installer before you commit.",
    "description": "Champion's fleX line is its newest home standby range, and the 201614 pairs the 26kW generator (model 201244) with a 200-amp service-entrance automatic transfer switch (model 201039). Champion rates it at 26,000 watts on propane and 23,400 watts on natural gas from a 999cc V-twin, a slightly larger natural gas derate than Generac or Kohler but well clear of the older 22.5kW figures. Listed fuel use is 197.8 cubic feet per hour of natural gas at half load and 321.1 at full load, and 2.39 gallons of propane per hour at half load and 3.95 at full load. The noise rating is listed at 67 to 68 dB(A) at 7 meters, with Champion describing the figure as approximate. The switch's response time is adjustable from 2.5 to 240 seconds, which lets an installer ride through brief utility blips instead of starting the engine for every flicker. The headline is the warranty: 10 years limited on the generator, against 5 for Generac and Kohler and 7 for Briggs & Stratton. The caveat is that the transfer switch is covered for only 2 years, and Champion's standby dealer network is much thinner than Generac's. Retailer listings have put the package at about $7,499. A long warranty is only as useful as the nearest technician willing to honor it, so get a local installer's name before choosing this one."
  },
  {
    "id": "generac-7326-24kw",
    "name": "Generac 7326 24kW Home Standby Generator with 200A Transfer Switch",
    "category": "Next Gen Air-Cooled, 24kW LP / 22.5kW NG",
    "badge": "Best 24kW",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "22.5kW on natural gas, the same NG rating the older Guardian 26kW carried",
      "Retailer listings give 197 cfh of natural gas at half load and 291 at full load",
      "200-amp service-entrance transfer switch and cellular monitoring included",
      "Usually a few hundred dollars less than the 26kW package"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1,500 fewer watts on natural gas than the 26kW Next Gen unit",
      "Same 5-year warranty and subscription caveats as the 7328"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If your load calculation lands at 20kW or under on natural gas, this is the better buy than the 26kW: the same switch and platform for less.",
    "description": "The 7326 is the 24kW model in Generac's current Next Gen line, packaged with a 200-amp service-entrance transfer switch and cellular connectivity. It is rated 24,000 watts on propane and 22,500 watts on natural gas, which puts it exactly where the outgoing Guardian 26kW sat on natural gas. Retailer spec listings give 197 cubic feet per hour of natural gas at half load and 291 at full load, and 2.45 gallons of propane per hour at half load and 3.71 at full load. For a house whose real peak demand, after a proper load calculation, comes in around 18 to 20kW, the 7326 is the smarter purchase than the 26kW: it uses the same enclosure, controller and switch, and the money saved can go to a load management module that does more for a two-compressor house than another 1,500 watts. It is also the size to consider when the natural gas meter is marginal, because it draws less at full load. Choose the 26kW instead if the calculation is already near 22kW, if you have an electric range and an electric water heater you want live, or if you are adding an EV charger or a heat pump later. Our <a href=\"/generac-generator-cost/\">Generac cost guide</a> covers how prices step between adjacent sizes."
  },
  {
    "id": "generac-7329-28kw",
    "name": "Generac 7329 28kW Home Standby Generator with 200A Transfer Switch",
    "category": "Next Gen Air-Cooled, 28kW LP / 25kW NG",
    "badge": "Step-Up Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Generac markets it as the most powerful air-cooled home standby generator available",
      "Retailers list 28kW on propane and 25kW on natural gas",
      "Hydraulic valve lifters, a first for a Generac air-cooled unit",
      "Stays in the air-cooled price and maintenance tier rather than jumping to a liquid-cooled unit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only worth the premium if a load calculation genuinely exceeds 24kW on natural gas",
      "Newer model with limited long-term owner history"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The answer when 26kW is close but not quite enough. It avoids the cost jump to a liquid-cooled 32kW-plus unit.",
    "description": "Generac announced its 28kW air-cooled unit in January 2025 and sells it as the 7282 (generator only) and the 7329 (with a 200-amp service-entrance transfer switch). Retail listings give 28kW on propane and 25kW on natural gas, and Generac describes it as the most powerful air-cooled home standby generator on the market. Its engine gets hydraulic valve lifters, which Generac says is a first for its air-cooled line. Retail listings give about 182 cubic feet per hour of natural gas at half load and 297 at full load, and 2.23 gallons of propane per hour at half load and 3.97 at full load; Generac says such figures are approximate and the unit's fuel decal is the final word. This is on the list as the ceiling of the class rather than a default. If a load calculation puts your house at 24 to 25kW on natural gas, the alternatives are a liquid-cooled unit at roughly double the price, which <a href=\"/best-whole-house-generator/\">our whole house generator guide</a> covers, or this. If the calculation lands under 22kW, the 26kW or even the 24kW model does the job for less, and more load management beats more engine for most two-compressor houses."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best 26kW Generators",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-26kw-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 26kW generator big enough for a whole house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes with a 200-amp service, yes. A typical 200-amp house with two air conditioners and gas heat draws 9 to 15kW of running load, well inside the 24kW natural gas rating of current 26kW units. All-electric homes with heat pumps, electric cooking and an EV charger can exceed it and need load management or a larger unit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a 26kW generator run two air conditioners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, yes. Two 3 to 4 ton systems run at roughly 7 to 8kW combined, which a 26kW unit carries easily. The challenge is both compressors starting at once. A load manager on the second unit, or soft starters on both condensers, prevents that and makes the generator's job much easier."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much natural gas does a 26kW generator use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturer figures run about 180 to 206 cubic feet per hour at half load and 290 to 323 at full load. That is roughly two therms an hour at half load. At an assumed $1.50 per therm, about $3 an hour. Your gas meter must handle the generator's full-load draw plus your other gas appliances."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a 26kW generator run on a 500-gallon propane tank?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 500-gallon tank is filled to about 80%, so roughly 400 gallons. At the 2.05 to 2.60 gallons per hour these units list at half load, that is about six to eight days. At full load, four to five days. These are calculations from rated fuel use, not measured runtimes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between the Generac 7291 and 7328?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both are 26kW on propane. The Guardian 7291 is the outgoing model, rated 22.5kW on natural gas with Wi-Fi monitoring. The Next Gen 7328 is rated 24kW on natural gas and uses built-in cellular monitoring. Both include a 200-amp transfer switch. On natural gas, the 7328 is the better unit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a 26kW generator cost installed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Retail listings put the Generac 7328 package at roughly $7,250 to $8,200 for the equipment. Published 2026 installer and cost-guide estimates for a complete 22 to 26kW natural gas installation commonly fall around $9,000 to $16,000, depending on gas and electrical run lengths, meter upgrades and permits. Get three itemized quotes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a 24kW or 26kW generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy the 24kW if your load calculation lands at or below about 20kW on natural gas or your gas meter is marginal. Buy the 26kW if it lands between 20 and 23kW, or you have two compressors and do not want to rely on load management. On natural gas the difference is 22.5kW versus 24kW."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "26kw generator",
      "26 kw generator",
      "26kw standby generator",
      "generac 26kw",
      "24kw generator"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">26kW Generators</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Six air-cooled standby units from 24kW to 28kW, compared on the number that matters most: what they actually make on natural gas.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#generac-7328-26kw"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#who-needs-26kw" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Who Needs 26kW</a>
          <a href="#ng-vs-lp" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. NG vs LP Ratings</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Picks</a>
          <a href="#200-amp-two-ac" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. 200A and Two ACs</a>
          <a href="#load-management" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Load Management</a>
          <a href="#fuel-use" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Fuel Use</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Installed Cost</a>
          <a href="#24-26-28" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. 24 vs 26 vs 28kW</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Installation</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. When It's Wrong</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every 26kW generator makes 26kW on propane. On natural gas, the current models range from 22.5kW to 24kW, and that gap, plus how well a unit starts two air conditioners at once, is what separates them.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="who-needs-26kw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Actually Needs a 26kW Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 26kW generator is the largest size most residential installers will quote before the price jumps to a liquid-cooled unit, and that is exactly why it gets oversold. It is the top of the air-cooled range for Generac, Kohler, Briggs & Stratton and Champion, a 999cc-class V-twin in an aluminum box about four feet long, and it costs a couple of thousand dollars more than a 22kW unit once the larger gas pipe and wiring are included. Buy it because a load calculation says you need it, not because a salesperson called it future-proofing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The houses that genuinely need 26kW share a profile. They have two central air conditioners or two heat pumps, and the owners want both running during a summer outage. They have electric cooking or an electric water heater on top of that. They run 3,500 square feet or more, or they have a well pump and a sump pump that can both start at the wrong moment. An EV charger that has to stay live, or a pool pump that cannot sit idle in August, pushes the number up further. If two of those describe your house, 26kW is a reasonable target.
          </p>

          <p>
            The houses that do not need it are more common. A 2,000 to 2,500 square foot home with one AC unit and gas appliances usually lands between 14 and 20kW of real demand, and our <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">generator sizing guide</a> walks through that math. A 22kW unit, or a 24kW unit with a load management module on the dryer and range, covers that house with room to spare. Spending the difference on a 26kW unit buys capacity that sits unused for the twenty-year life of the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            This page is narrower than <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">our whole house generator roundup</a>, which ranks standby units from 14kW to liquid-cooled 38kW. Here we stay inside the 24 to 28kW air-cooled class, because the decisions that matter at this size are different: natural gas derating, two-compressor starting, gas meter capacity, and whether load management lets you step down a size. Every unit below is a permanently installed automatic standby generator; if you are deciding between that and a large portable, start with <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby generators</a> first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ng-vs-lp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Headline Number Is a Propane Rating
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every 26kW generator on this page makes 26kW on propane only. On natural gas, which is how most suburban standby generators are fed, the rating drops because pipeline gas carries less energy per cubic foot. The size of that drop varies more than buyers expect, and it is the single most useful number to compare across brands. Kohler's spec sheet, Briggs & Stratton's 040806 sheet and Generac's page for its Next Gen 26kW all list 24kW on natural gas. Champion lists 23.4kW. Generac's outgoing Guardian 26kW, models 7290 and 7291, is rated 22.5kW on natural gas.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last point is worth acting on. The Guardian 7291 is still sold by some retailers and still appears in quotes, and it looks identical to the Next Gen 7328 on a sales sheet that only says 26kW. On natural gas it delivers 1,500 fewer watts, roughly the running load of a 3-ton air conditioner. If you are on a gas line, ask for the exact model number and the natural gas rating in writing before you sign. If the installer is quoting the older unit at a meaningful discount, that can still be a fair deal for a house that never needs more than 20kW, but it should be a choice, not a surprise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane owners get the full 26kW, but they trade it for a fuel supply they have to manage. A standby unit pulls vapor from the tank, and the tank has to be large enough to vaporize propane fast enough in cold weather, which is why installers usually specify a 250 to 500 gallon tank for this size rather than a pair of 100-pound cylinders. Our <a href="/best-propane-generator/">propane generator guide</a> covers the portable side of that trade-off; for a standby unit the propane supplier sizes the tank and regulator.
          </p>

