        <div id="200-amp-two-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does 26kW Cover a 200-Amp Service With Two AC Units?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually yes, with a caveat about starting rather than running. A 200-amp service at 240 volts is a 48kW ceiling on paper, but no house draws that. The panel rating is sized for code, not for the real simultaneous load, and a typical 200-amp home with two air conditioners peaks somewhere between 15 and 22kW when everything that actually runs at once is added up. A 26kW generator rated 24kW on natural gas can carry that running load. The real question is what happens when both compressors try to start at the same moment.
          </p>

          <p>
            A residential compressor draws several times its running current for a fraction of a second as it starts, and two starting together is the hardest thing a standby generator does. This is what motor-starting ratings describe. Briggs & Stratton lists 65.5 kVA of motor starting at a 35% voltage dip for its 26kW unit; Kohler lists 39 kVA at the same dip for the 26RCA. Neither number means the other unit fails, but it tells you which one has more headroom when the house is already loaded and a second compressor kicks on.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical fix costs far less than the next size up. A soft starter on each AC condenser cuts the starting surge substantially and is a common add-on at install. Load management, covered in the next section, keeps the two compressors from starting at the same instant. With either one in place, a 26kW unit handles a 200-amp house with two 3 to 4 ton systems comfortably. Without them, it usually still works, but with a deeper flicker and more strain on the alternator than necessary.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A rough running-load budget</h3>

          <p>
            For a 200-amp house with two 3-ton air conditioners, gas heat and gas hot water, a realistic simultaneous running load looks like this: about 3.5kW per compressor including the air handler, so 7kW for both; 1kW for refrigerators and freezers; 1 to 2kW for lighting, electronics and a well or sump pump cycling; and the occasional 4 to 5kW spike from an electric dryer or oven. That totals 9 to 15kW running, well inside a 24kW natural gas rating. Our <a href="/how-many-watts-to-power-a-house/">household wattage guide</a> has the per-appliance figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where 26kW starts to run out is the all-electric house: two heat pumps with electric resistance backup strips, an electric range, an electric water heater and an EV charger can push simultaneous demand past 30kW. That house either sheds loads with management modules or moves to the 28kW unit or a liquid-cooled generator. If you are not sure which group you are in, an installer's load calculation is part of every proper quote, and you should ask to see it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="load-management" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Load Management: Making 26kW Act Like More
          </h2>

          <p>
            Load management is the reason a 26kW generator can back up a house whose appliances add up to 40kW on paper. A load manager sits between the transfer switch and a big appliance, monitors the generator's frequency or output, and switches that appliance off when the generator nears its limit, then back on when there is room. During normal grid operation it does nothing. During an outage, it means the dryer waits while both air conditioners run, or the second compressor waits until the first has started.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each brand handles it slightly differently. Generac sells separate load management modules, including a 50-amp load manager (model 7000) for 240-volt loads such as an AC condenser or range. Kohler builds a combined interface and load management board into the RXT transfer switch that comes with the 26RCAL, controlled from the generator's RDC2 controller. Briggs & Stratton and Champion offer their own modules. Every one of them is a relay that cuts power to a circuit, so the installer has to wire each managed load through one.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a two-compressor house, the most valuable single module is on the second air conditioner. It stops the two units from starting together, which removes the worst surge the generator ever sees, and the house barely notices the delay. The second most valuable is on an electric range or dryer, which are large, intermittent loads that rarely need to run during an outage at the same time as everything else. A smart thermostat can do some of the same work by staggering cooling calls, but it is not a substitute for a relay on the condenser.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Price a load manager before you price a bigger engine.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your load calculation lands at 22 to 24kW, two load management modules on a 22kW or 24kW unit often cost less than stepping up to 26kW and do more to protect the alternator. Ask your installer to quote both ways.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The trade-off is honesty about what will run. A managed house is a house where some things wait. If your household will not tolerate the dryer pausing during a heat wave, budget for the larger generator instead. For most families, a few minutes of delay on a non-essential circuit is an easy trade for a smaller, cheaper machine that burns less fuel every hour it runs. Our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch guide</a> explains how service-entrance switches and load shedding fit together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel Use on Natural Gas and Propane
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel is the running cost buyers underestimate, and at this size it is significant. Manufacturer figures for the 26kW class cluster around 180 to 206 cubic feet per hour of natural gas at half load and 290 to 323 at full load. On propane, the same units list about 2.0 to 2.6 gallons per hour at half load and 3.5 to 4.7 at full load. Half load is the honest planning figure for most outages, because a house with the air conditioners cycling rarely holds the generator at full output for long.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural gas is cheap enough at those rates that most owners never think about it. One hundred cubic feet of natural gas is roughly one therm, so a 26kW unit at half load burns about two therms an hour. At an assumed $1.50 per therm, that is about $3 an hour, or $72 a day. Check your own utility bill for the actual rate; it varies by region and season. The constraint on natural gas is not cost but supply: the generator plus the furnace, water heater and range all have to fit through your gas meter and service line, which the installation section below covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane is where fuel use decides how long you can stay powered. A 500-gallon tank is filled to about 80%, so it holds roughly 400 usable gallons. At 2.05 gallons per hour, the Generac 7328's listed half-load figure, that is about 195 hours, or eight days. At the Briggs & Stratton's 2.60 gallons per hour, it is about 154 hours, closer to six and a half days. At full load, any of these units would empty the same tank in four to five days. A 250-gallon tank halves all of those figures.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">500-Gallon Propane Tank, Roughly 400 Usable Gallons</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~195 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Generac 7328 at half load (2.05 gal/hr)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~167 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Champion 201614 at half load (2.39 gal/hr)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~154 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Briggs 040806 at half load (2.60 gal/hr)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~101 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Generac 7328 at full load (3.95 gal/hr)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those runtimes are arithmetic on manufacturer figures, not measurements, and real consumption moves with the load. At an assumed $3 per gallon, the 7328 at half load costs about $6 an hour in propane, twice the natural gas figure. The fuel case for load management is direct: every kilowatt you do not run is fuel you do not burn. The 90-second weekly exercise Kohler uses by default, and the short quiet-test modes on the others, also keep the routine fuel bill for exercising small.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What a 26kW Generator Costs Installed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Equipment first. Generac lists a starting MSRP of $6,989 for the outgoing Guardian 26kW 7290 on its own site. Retail listings at AP Electric and Lowe's put the Next Gen 7328 package, with its 200-amp transfer switch, at roughly $7,250 to $8,200. Retailer listings have put the Champion 201614 package at about $7,499. Kohler and Briggs & Stratton 26kW packages are sold mainly through dealers and are typically quoted with installation, so a bare equipment price is harder to pin down. Our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac generator cost guide</a> lists a $6,800 to $7,800 range for the Guardian 26kW unit and explains why the step from 22kW to 26kW is larger than the steps below it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation is where the totals diverge. Installer and cost-guide estimates published for 2026 commonly put a complete 22 to 26kW natural gas installation in the region of $9,000 to $16,000, with the high end for long gas and electrical runs, meter upgrades or service-entrance work. Treat that as a planning range, not a quote; two houses with the same generator can differ by thousands depending on how far the unit sits from the meter and the panel. Our <a href="/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/">whole house generator installation cost guide</a> breaks down each line item.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 26kW-specific costs are mostly about the larger fuel demand. A 26kW unit needs roughly 290,000 to 330,000 BTU per hour of natural gas at full load, which often means a larger gas pipe than a 22kW unit on the same run, and sometimes a meter upgrade from the utility. The electrical side needs heavier conductors for a roughly 100 to 109-amp generator output. On propane, the tank and its installation are a separate bill from the propane supplier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ongoing costs are modest but real: an annual service visit with oil, filter and spark plugs, a battery every few years, and the fuel for weekly exercise. Extended warranties are worth pricing at purchase, because most must be bought within the first year. Kohler's sheet lists optional 7 and 10-year coverage, and Champion's 10-year limited warranty is standard. Generac and Kohler start at 5 years; Briggs & Stratton includes 7.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="24-26-28" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. 24kW, 26kW or 28kW: Picking the Rung
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generac now sells three adjacent air-cooled sizes at the top of its range, and the right one depends on two numbers: your calculated peak load on the fuel you actually have, and your gas meter capacity. On natural gas, the 7326 makes 22.5kW, the 7328 makes 24kW and the 7329 makes 25kW. The steps are smaller than the model names suggest. Going from 24kW to 28kW on the label buys 2.5kW on natural gas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose 24kW if the load calculation lands at or below about 20kW on natural gas, or if your gas meter is marginal. It is the same platform and switch as the 26kW for less money, and it draws less gas at full load. Choose 26kW if the calculation is between 20 and 23kW, or if you have two compressors and do not want to rely on load management. It is the most popular size in this class for good reason: it covers almost every gas-heated 200-amp house without shedding anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose 28kW only if the calculation exceeds about 23kW on natural gas and you want to stay air-cooled. It is a newer product with less owner history, but it sits in the same maintenance tier as the others and avoids the cost and service demands of a liquid-cooled generator. If the calculation is above 25kW, the honest answer is either aggressive load management or a liquid-cooled unit; an air-cooled engine run near its limit for days is working harder than it was designed to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kohler, Briggs & Stratton and Champion each top out at 26kW in their air-cooled residential ranges, so if a non-Generac installer is your best local option, the choice is simpler: 26kW with load management, or their smaller 20 to 22kW unit with more of it. On propane, every option here gains its full rated output, so a propane house can often step down a size compared with a natural gas house of the same load.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Installation Details That Decide Whether It Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            The gas meter is the most common surprise at this size. Many residential gas meters are rated around 250 cubic feet per hour, and a 26kW generator alone can draw more than 300 at full load, before the furnace and water heater are counted. A proper installer checks the meter rating and the service pressure as part of the quote and arranges an upgrade with the utility if needed. If a quote does not mention the meter, ask, because a meter that cannot keep up shows up as a generator that stalls or surges under load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pipe size follows from the same numbers. Kohler's spec sheet recommends 1-1/4 inch pipe for natural gas runs of 50 to 150 feet and 1-1/2 inch for 200 feet, and a 3/4-inch inlet at the unit is only the connection size, not the supply size. Undersized pipe is a common cause of a standby generator that tests fine at no load during the weekly exercise and then struggles in a real outage. Put the generator as close to the meter as clearance rules allow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearance and placement come next. Kohler says its enclosure meets NFPA guidance for 18 inches to combustible materials, but local codes and the manufacturer's installation manual set the real distances from windows, doors, vents and property lines. Exhaust is the risk to plan around, and our <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">generator placement guide</a> covers the general rules. The units here weigh about 520 to 540 pounds, so they need a pad or a compacted gravel base, and in storm-prone areas Kohler recommends a 4-inch concrete pad.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the transfer switch. A whole-house 26kW installation normally uses a 200-amp service-entrance-rated automatic transfer switch, installed at the meter, so that the entire panel is backed up. The Generac, Kohler and Champion packages here include one. The Briggs & Stratton 040806 is the generator only, so the switch should be a separate, named line item on the quote. Surge protection is worth adding if the package lacks it; our <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector guide</a> covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When 26kW Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your outages are short and infrequent, a 26kW standby generator is a lot of machine for a few hours a year. A battery system can carry a house through a typical afternoon outage silently, with a switchover fast enough that clocks do not reset, and our <a href="/whole-house-ups/">whole-house UPS guide</a> explains when that beats an engine and when it does not. Battery systems struggle with multi-day outages and large air conditioning loads, which is exactly where a 26kW generator earns its price.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your house fits in 20kW, a smaller standby unit is simply cheaper to buy, install and fuel. If you want the most power without permanent installation, the largest portable generators reach about 20,000 watts on gasoline, and our <a href="/best-20000-watt-generator/">20,000-watt generator guide</a> covers that end. A large portable needs a transfer switch or interlock, someone to start and refuel it, and a safe place outside to run it, so it is a different trade: lower cost and more effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the real goal is just keeping a furnace, a refrigerator and a sump pump running, both a 26kW standby and a whole-house battery are overkill. A mid-size portable or dual-fuel generator with a transfer switch does that for a fraction of the price; our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide for power outages</a> is the place to start. Buy a 26kW unit when you want the entire house, including both air conditioners, running automatically for days. That is what it does better than anything else at this price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing 26kW Labels Instead of Natural Gas Ratings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every unit here is 26kW on propane. On natural gas they range from 22.5kW on the outgoing Generac Guardian 7291 to 24kW on the Generac 7328, Kohler 26RCAL and Briggs 040806. If you are on a gas line, compare the natural gas number in writing, because that is the one your house will actually get.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Gas Meter Check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 26kW unit can draw over 300 cubic feet of natural gas per hour at full load, on top of the furnace and water heater. Many residential meters are rated near 250. A quote that never mentions the meter or the pipe size is a quote that may end with a generator that surges under load.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 26kW Instead of Load Management</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If your load calculation lands at 22 to 24kW, two load managers on a smaller unit often cost less than the size step and protect the alternator better. Price both before signing; the bigger engine burns more fuel every hour of every outage for twenty years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Motor-Starting Capability</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two compressors starting at once is the hardest job a standby generator does. Spec sheets publish motor-starting kVA for a reason: Briggs lists 65.5 kVA and Kohler 39 kVA at the same voltage dip. Ask for the figure, and add soft starters or load management if it looks thin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Propane Tank Math</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At about 2 to 2.6 gallons per hour at half load, a 500-gallon tank holding 400 usable gallons lasts six to eight days. A 250-gallon tank lasts half that. Size the tank for the outage you are planning for, and confirm the supplier will deliver during a storm.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 26kW generator big enough for a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes with a 200-amp service, yes. A typical 200-amp house with two air conditioners and gas heat draws 9 to 15kW of running load, well inside the 24kW natural gas rating of current 26kW units. All-electric homes with heat pumps, electric cooking and an EV charger can exceed it and need load management or a larger unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 26kW generator run two air conditioners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Two 3 to 4 ton systems run at roughly 7 to 8kW combined, which a 26kW unit carries easily. The challenge is both compressors starting at once. A load manager on the second unit, or soft starters on both condensers, prevents that and makes the generator's job much easier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much natural gas does a 26kW generator use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturer figures run about 180 to 206 cubic feet per hour at half load and 290 to 323 at full load. That is roughly two therms an hour at half load. At an assumed $1.50 per therm, about $3 an hour. Your gas meter must handle the generator's full-load draw plus your other gas appliances.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 26kW generator run on a 500-gallon propane tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 500-gallon tank is filled to about 80%, so roughly 400 gallons. At the 2.05 to 2.60 gallons per hour these units list at half load, that is about six to eight days. At full load, four to five days. These are calculations from rated fuel use, not measured runtimes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Generac 7291 and 7328?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are 26kW on propane. The Guardian 7291 is the outgoing model, rated 22.5kW on natural gas with Wi-Fi monitoring. The Next Gen 7328 is rated 24kW on natural gas and uses built-in cellular monitoring. Both include a 200-amp transfer switch. On natural gas, the 7328 is the better unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a 26kW generator cost installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Retail listings put the Generac 7328 package at roughly $7,250 to $8,200 for the equipment. Published 2026 installer and cost-guide estimates for a complete 22 to 26kW natural gas installation commonly fall around $9,000 to $16,000, depending on gas and electrical run lengths, meter upgrades and permits. Get three itemized quotes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a 24kW or 26kW generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy the 24kW if your load calculation lands at or below about 20kW on natural gas or your gas meter is marginal. Buy the 26kW if it lands between 20 and 23kW, or you have two compressors and do not want to rely on load management. On natural gas the difference is 22.5kW versus 24kW.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

